========================================================================================
(A) is the per-subject optimum reliable at all?
========================================================================================

Split each subject's trials into random halves, estimate the optimum
independently in each, correlate across subjects. 200 random splits.

estimator                         subjects   split-half r           95% range  full-length
quadratic vertex [all trials]           16         +0.033      [-0.59, +0.78]       +0.064
quadratic vertex [hard course]           0           +nan        [+nan, +nan]         +nan
top-quartile arousal [all trials]        16         +0.703      [+0.52, +0.86]       +0.826
top-quartile arousal [hard course]        16         +0.714      [+0.54, +0.83]       +0.833
performance-weighted arousal [all trials]        16         +0.888      [+0.79, +0.95]       +0.941
performance-weighted arousal [hard course]        16         +0.794      [+0.66, +0.90]       +0.885

A reliability near zero means the per-subject optimum is mostly noise,
and no baseline measure could predict it however well chosen.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Is that reliability anything more than the subject's overall level?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  correlation with that subject's mean arousal, across subjects:
     quadratic vertex                 r = -0.668  (p = 0.025)
     top-quartile arousal             r = +0.939  (p = 7e-08)
     performance-weighted arousal     r = +0.987  (p = 1.4e-12)

  split-half reliability of subject MEAN arousal, which uses no
  performance information at all: r = +0.917 (full-length +0.957)

  reliability of each optimum after subtracting that subject's mean:
     quadratic vertex                 r = +0.226  95% [-0.41, +0.91]
     top-quartile arousal             r = +0.017  95% [-0.37, +0.45]
     performance-weighted arousal     r = +0.141  95% [-0.21, +0.48]

  If the residualised reliability includes zero, the stable individual
  difference is arousal LEVEL, not an individual optimum - and the
  per-subject rescaling in the decoder already normalises level.

